---
description: Enforces a strict Git commit message format using emojis, conventional commit types, and scopes.
alwaysApply: true
---
# Git Commit Guidelines

When generating or suggesting git commit messages, strictly follow the format:

Header: `<emoji> <type>(<scope>): <subject>`
Body(optional): `<freetext>`
Footer(optional): `<issue>:<space>` or `<space>#<code>`

## Examples:
✨ feat(auth): add google login functionality
🐛 fix(users): resolve profile picture upload error
🎨 style(nav): adjust header padding for responsiveness
📚 docs(readme): update API setup instructions
♻️ refactor(api): simplify database connection logic
⚡ perf(images): implement lazy loading for gallery
🧪 test(checkout): add unit tests for discount codes
🧹 chore(deps): upgrade typescript to latest version
⏪ revert(cart): roll back checkout page redesign

## Constraints:
1. **Header format**: Must start with an emoji, followed by the Conventional Commit type, the scope, and a concise message.
2. **Body**: Add a brief, bulleted explanation of "what" and "why" if the changes are complex.
3. **Length**: First line (header) must be under 50 characters.

## Commit generation — read all staged files
When drafting a commit message (or creating a commit), **do not** rely only on `git status` or a truncated summary. Always inspect **every staged** file:

1. Run `git status` and `git diff --cached` to list only staged modified, added, and deleted paths. Ignore unstaged and untracked files.
2. **Read each staged file in full** (or its full staged diff hunks) — including multiple files across frontend, backend, and shared paths — before writing the subject/body.
3. If the staged set spans many files, process them all; do not sample only the first few or the currently open file.
4. Derive the `<type>`, `<scope>`, and `<subject>` from the combined intent of **all staged** files, not from a single file.
5. Prefer one commit that accurately covers the staged multi-file set; if unrelated concerns appear across staged files, call that out and split commits only when the user asks.

### Specification Details

The key words “MUST”, “MUST NOT”, “REQUIRED”, “SHALL”, “SHALL NOT”, “SHOULD”, “SHOULD NOT”, “RECOMMENDED”, “MAY”, and “OPTIONAL” in this document are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119.

Commits MUST be prefixed with a type, which consists of a noun, feat, fix, etc., followed by the OPTIONAL scope, OPTIONAL !, and REQUIRED terminal colon and space.
The type feat MUST be used when a commit adds a new feature to your application or library.
The type fix MUST be used when a commit represents a bug fix for your application.
A scope MAY be provided after a type. A scope MUST consist of a noun describing a section of the codebase surrounded by parenthesis, e.g., fix(parser):
A description MUST immediately follow the colon and space after the type/scope prefix. The description is a short summary of the code changes, e.g., fix: array parsing issue when multiple spaces were contained in string.
A longer commit body MAY be provided after the short description, providing additional contextual information about the code changes. The body MUST begin one blank line after the description.
A commit body is free-form and MAY consist of any number of newline separated paragraphs.
One or more footers MAY be provided one blank line after the body. Each footer MUST consist of a word token, followed by either a :<space> or <space># separator, followed by a string value (this is inspired by the git trailer convention).
A footer’s token MUST use - in place of whitespace characters, e.g., Acked-by (this helps differentiate the footer section from a multi-paragraph body). An exception is made for BREAKING CHANGE, which MAY also be used as a token.
A footer’s value MAY contain spaces and newlines, and parsing MUST terminate when the next valid footer token/separator pair is observed.
Breaking changes MUST be indicated in the type/scope prefix of a commit, or as an entry in the footer.
If included as a footer, a breaking change MUST consist of the uppercase text BREAKING CHANGE, followed by a colon, space, and description, e.g., BREAKING CHANGE: environment variables now take precedence over config files.
If included in the type/scope prefix, breaking changes MUST be indicated by a ! immediately before the :. If ! is used, BREAKING CHANGE: MAY be omitted from the footer section, and the commit description SHALL be used to describe the breaking change.
Types other than feat and fix MAY be used in your commit messages, e.g., docs: update ref docs.
The units of information that make up Conventional Commits MUST NOT be treated as case sensitive by implementors, with the exception of BREAKING CHANGE which MUST be uppercase.
BREAKING-CHANGE MUST be synonymous with BREAKING CHANGE, when used as a token in a footer.
